{
  "dom_id": "step-q104290",
  "name": "Question 3",
  "problem": "$$\nf(r,\\theta )=\u27e8r\\operatorname{cos}\u2061\\theta ,\\,r\\operatorname{sin}\u2061\\theta ,\\,\\text{_____}\u27e9\n$$\n\nThe vector-valued function $f(r,\\theta )$ parametrizes the surface defined in cylindrical polar coordinates as \n$4{r}^{2}-{z}^{2}=0$\nfor $z\\ge 0.$ What is the third component of $f?$",
  "worked_solution": "First, we write the equation of the surface in the form $z=f(r,\\theta ):$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}4{r}^{2}-{z}^{2} & =0 \\\\ {z}^{2} & =4{r}^{2} \\\\ z & =\\sqrt{4{r}^{2}} \\\\ z & =2r\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe disregard the negative solution since we are given that $z\\ge 0.$\n\nIf $(r,\\theta ,z)$ are the cylindrical coordinates of a point and $(x,y,z)$ are the corresponding Cartesian coordinates of that point, then we have\n\n$$\nx=r\\operatorname{cos}\u2061\\theta ,\\,y=r\\operatorname{sin}\u2061\\theta ,\\,z=z.\n$$\n\nNow, a point $(x,y,z)$ on our surface has the corresponding position vector given by\n\n$$\nf=\u27e8x,\\,y,\\,z\u27e9=\u27e8r\\operatorname{cos}\u2061\\theta ,\\,r\\operatorname{sin}\u2061\\theta ,\\,z\u27e9.\n$$\n\nFinally, substituting $z=2r,$ we obtain\n\n$$\nf(r,\\theta )=\u27e8r\\operatorname{cos}\u2061\\theta ,\\,r\\operatorname{sin}\u2061\\theta ,\\,2r\u27e9.\n$$\n\nTherefore, the third component of $f$ is $2r.$",
